# Recording Finished Work (harness rule 12 — full detail)

Read before recording completed work, or when deciding whether work is
"done."

## The invariants

Whatever system you use, three things must be true:

1. **Finished work leaves a durable, referenceable record.** Not "the files
   are on disk" — a record with an identifier another agent can cite later.
   Here: a git commit, referenced by its hash (short hash is fine) — **or**,
   when the pass produced no project-repo diff (review-only, a bounce-back,
   a decision with no code to show for it), the literal token
   `no code changes`. Either way, that reference is recorded in two places:
   `.friday/active/harness/status_history.md` and next to the task in
   `.friday/active/harness/coding/tasks_finished.md`.
2. **The record is attributable to the role that did the work.** Two agents'
   changes must never sit mingled in one unrecorded blob — an
   un-attributable working state turns a one-line revert into archaeology.
3. **A task is not `[DONE]` until its record exists and its reference is
   pasted next to the task** in `.friday/active/harness/coding/tasks_finished.md`.

## Who records, and when

**The Reviewer commits everything at close-out.** No other role commits.
Working state between passes stays uncommitted (or in the Reviewer's own
staging) — this keeps history clean at the cost of the Reviewer needing to
attribute correctly (see below): never sign a commit as itself when it's
recording another role's work.

## What the Reviewer commits

Since v0.13.0, harness working state (`.friday/active/harness/status.md`,
`plans/`, `coding/tasks_*.md`, etc.) lives in `.friday/active/` — a
directory that belongs to neither the project repo nor `.friday` itself,
tracked by nothing. A close-out commit in the project repo can therefore
only ever contain **project-repo content**: source, tests, and `docs/`.
Harness state is never staged — it is not in this repo, and there is
nothing to `git add` there even by mistake as long as you scope your
commits as below.

- Before committing, check `git status --porcelain -- . ':!.friday'`. If
  that is empty, the pass produced no project-repo diff — **make no
  commit.** Record the literal token `no code changes` in
  `tasks_finished.md` and `status_history.md` instead (still write the
  commit-message-shaped record described below into those files' notes,
  so the directive/tracker trail survives even without a commit).
- If it's non-empty, commit with `git commit -- . ':!.friday'` —
  **always pass this pathspec explicitly.** Without it, a `.friday`
  submodule-pointer bump sitting in the working tree rides along silently
  inside a `Reviewer:` commit. A pointer bump is a legitimate project-repo
  change (it IS tracked, unlike everything under `.friday/active/`), but it
  is a distinct kind of change and belongs in its own `Harness:`-attributed
  commit, never folded into a `Reviewer:` close-out.

## Attribution format

Every commit message starts with `Role: description` — capitalized role
name exactly as it appears in `.friday/active/harness/harness.md` ("Roles:" line: Planner,
Coder, Runner, Reviewer, Author, Researcher; Controller does not itself
produce recordable work), colon, space, then a normal imperative summary.
The Reviewer attributes to whichever role actually did the work being
committed, never to itself unless the Reviewer's own edit (e.g. a
close-out note) is what's being recorded.

Examples:
- `Coder: implement incremental state update in the core pipeline`
- `Researcher: draft formal model memo for the trust-scoring approach`
- `Reviewer: close directive 3, verify eval provenance`

**Reviewer commits carry a mandatory body**, because the commit message is
now the durable record of *why* — `status_history.md` isn't tracked by
either repo (see the durability note below). The body must include:
- a directive reference: `Directive: <id>` (or an inline `directive <id>`
  mention), and
- a tracker reference: the issue/MR (`#123`, `!45`, `ABC-12`) this closes,
  or the literal `no tracker` if the project has none configured.

Worked examples:

```
Reviewer: close directive D-12, verify eval provenance

Directive: D-12
Tracker: #123
```

```
Reviewer: bounce review-only pass back with no project-repo diff, no code changes

Directive: D-14
no tracker
```

## Prohibitions

- Never force-push.
- Never commit `.env`, `harness.config.env`, or any credential/token file.
