## Mechanical backstop

Both adapters (`.claude/hooks/check_commit_msg.py` and
`.agents/hooks/check_commit_msg.py`) symlink to the same shared
implementation, wired via each adapter's `commit-msg` hook, which itself
symlinks to `.git/hooks/commit-msg` — warn-only, exits 0 always, exempts
merges and `fixup!`/`squash!` commits. It runs two checks:

1. **Role-prefix check (all commits):** anything whose first line doesn't
   match `ROLE_PREFIX_RE` (Planner, Coder, Runner, Reviewer, Author,
   Researcher, Controller, Harness) is flagged, never rejected.
2. **Reviewer body check (Reviewer commits only):** when the first line's
   role is `Reviewer`, the body (whole message, minus git's own trailing
   `#`-comment block) is checked for a directive reference and a tracker
   reference per "Attribution format" above. Missing either prints a WARN;
   it still never blocks — `commit-msg` is symlinked straight into
   `.git/hooks/commit-msg`, and a blocking check here would strand a
   Reviewer mid-pass with no escape hatch.

## Durability note

`status_history.md` (like the rest of `.friday/active/`) is tracked by
**neither** the project repo nor `.friday` — a fresh clone of either will
never have it. Treat it as a convenience index, not a source of truth: the
genuinely durable carriers of a close-out are the **commit message** (this
file's format above) and the **tracker issue**, both of which live in
systems that survive a clone. A future reader reconstructing history should
follow commit hashes and tracker references, not `status_history.md`.

## Remote

`origin` → `[SET AT SETUP: VCS_REMOTE]` (SSH, private repo). Commits happen
at Reviewer close-out per above; pushing to `origin` is a separate, visible
action — confirm with the user before pushing unless a given session has
already authorized it for that pass.
